  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1223 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nSignificant drying of the atmosphere is forecast across NM going \ninto Saturday, with ridging aloft as the retrograding upper low \npulls up stationary offshore of the Baja Peninsula and wraps \nmoisture from Tropical Storm Simon into it's circulation. Near-\nideal radiational cooling conditions Friday night into Saturday\nmorning with a very dry airmass in place will bring an even colder\nnight with a hard freeze forecast across much of the area. A\nFreeze Watch will be required for the east central/southeast\nplains and perhaps the middle RGV for Friday night as those \nforecast zones experience their 1st freeze of the season. The Baja\nlow is forecast to fill and open up while lifting northeast as a\nshortwave trough from Sunday into Monday, bringing moisture and \nclouds to NM but with low chances for precipitation due to very \nweak forcing associated with the trough. All eyes turn to the west\ncoast Tuesday as the jet stream dives south and brings a stronger\nupper low into SoCal by Wednesday. Moisture advection will be the\nrule across NM going into mid week, with increasing precipitation\nchances from mid through late week as the upper low swings east\nacross the region. There are still some timing differences with\nregard to the upper low passage over the region late next week,\nbut not as disparate as previous model cycles. Thursday looks to\nbe the wettest day next week across NM, with 700mb temperatures\nsupportive of snow across the mountain peaks.",
